	<!-- custom: ice feature and terrain have the same name which is very
	confusing, when navigating the sevopedia for example or even in game.
	After a bit of research i mean, i found this quite helpful for example:
	https://www.antarctica.gov.au/about-antarctica/ice-and-atmosphere/
	Based on this, ice water would be "sea ice", and as for ice land, after
	more research as explained at the TXT_KEY_TERRAIN_ICE's code comment
	(after this first TXT_KEY_FEATURE_ICE's comments), it seems that the types
	of ice in the world are more diverse than what this document about
	antarctica (only?) suggests/shows rather maybe, so i have decided to not
	change ice land, but only change ice water, see at the ice land part of
	the code details if need be i mean anyways..
	To be back about "sea ice" first, i also confirmed it here, if i am not
	mistaken i mean anyways: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ice#In_the_oceans
	Due to the technicality of the terms, i did not translate them but went
	to a wiki or something to move from one page in a language to the same
	page in another language.
	Hopefully this approach to renaming ice is accurate enough as i don't know
	a lot about ice (but read quite a bit now i mean), but most importantly,
	maybe it helps clear the confusion existing about Ice/Water and Ice/Land (
	note: be careful if ending line here, vs code creates transforms the word
	Land into a <Land></Land> tag)
    both being shown as "Ice", hence not being able to understand the
	different properties of each in sevopedia for example (not being able
	to settle on Ice/Water for example but possible in Ice/Land if i am not
	mistaken).
	About the singular and plural forms, i found the information here about
	the <Plural>0:1</Plural> tag:
	https://modiki.civfanatics.com/index.php/Civ4_XML_Text
	To quote it:
	"Plural (optional) - 0 = singular, 1 = plural. Many text keys have 0:1
	to include both singular and plural. Usually used where the plural is
	something different than an 's' added to the end."
	End of quotation i mean anyways.
	About sea ice: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sea_ice i mean; i took the
	translations from there, not from a translator i mean. And after having
	the word equivalent in the specific language i mean anyways, then i
	looked for dictionnaries listing the singular plural forms and gender
	i mean anyways if i could find it, still not using translators i mean
	anyways.-->
	<TEXT>
		<Tag>TXT_KEY_FEATURE_ICE</Tag>
		<English>Sea Ice</English>
